% ============================================================================
% joint-formed-by — the actual BONES that meet to form three synovial-joint
% types, from the NIH/NLM StatPearls page `joint-types.adj` also cites
% (adj-facts-stdlib, ANATOMY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `joint-types.adj`
% (`joint_example(joint_type, example)`, hinge/pivot/condyloid/saddle/planar/
% ball_and_socket → a representative joint the source names for each shape,
% e.g. pivot → atlantoaxial): the page that table cites also names, for three
% joint types, the specific BONES that meet to FORM the joint -- a fact the
% joint_type/example schema had no room for. (This paragraph used to say
% `joint-types.adj`'s header quotes these spans and that each names the
% example and the bones in the same sentence; that header no longer quotes
% them, and the saddle span takes three sentences, as noted below.)
%
%   pivot → atlas, axis
%     "The atlantoaxial joint, formed by the 1st (atlas) and 2nd (axis)
%      cervical vertebrae, is a pivot joint."
%
%   condyloid → distal_metacarpals, proximal_phalanges
%     "Examples of condyloid joints are the knuckles, formed by the distal
%      metacarpals and proximal phalanges of the medial 4 fingers."
%
%   saddle → trapezium, first_metacarpal
%     "the joint formed by the trapezium and 1st metacarpal bone" (a clause
%      of the last of the three sentences both saddle rows carry; see
%      "EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SPAN" below)
%
% New `joint_formed_by(joint_type, bone)` table decodes those named bones as
% each joint type's own rows (6 total, across the three joint types whose
% source span explicitly names the forming bones -- hinge, planar, and
% ball_and_socket are deliberately NOT rowed here, since their own quoted
% spans in `joint-types.adj` name only an example joint, never the specific
% bones that form it). "Bone" (what physically meets to form the joint) is
% categorically distinct from the parent table's "example" (the name of the
% whole joint itself, e.g. "atlantoaxial" or "knuckles") -- a joint's example
% name and its forming bones are two different facts about the same joint,
% and neither collapses into the other. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? joint_formed_by(pivot, $Bone)  % atlas, then axis
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like the
% parent's own): each `row` lowers to a ground relation `joint_formed_by
% (joint_type, bone)` carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the
% ordinary SLD binding query -- the engine returns the bone WITH its source,
% on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on anything that is not one
% of these three joint types (it never invents a forming bone). The query
% also runs BACKWARD -- bind a bone and recall the joint type it helps form:
%
%     ? joint_formed_by($T, trapezium)  % saddle
%
% A recall on `hinge` -- a real, already-tabled joint type in
% `joint-types.adj`, but whose own quoted span ("Examples include the elbow,
% knee, ankle, and interphalangeal joints.") names only example joints, never
% the specific bones that form any of them -- correctly abstains rather than
% inventing a bone for it.
%
% Provenance: the SAME NIH/NLM StatPearls "Anatomy, Joints" page that
% `joint-types.adj` cites. This paragraph used to say its spans were
% reproduced "byte-for-byte" with "no new WebFetch"; they are now measured
% against the page itself (below). `trust authoritative` -- the same tier
% the parent table already
% carries (StatPearls is a primary U.S. government NIH/NLM reference).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_no_byte_arithmetic_for_llm.)
%
% EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SPAN (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, #14986). The envelope used
% to be the pivot sentence, the primary source of all six answers, with the
% condyloid and saddle spans as table-level `cites`. Now:
%
%   - both pivot rows take the pivot sentence, which names the type and both
%     bones;
%   - both condyloid rows take the knuckles sentence, which names the type
%     and both bones;
%   - both saddle rows take the page's three contiguous sentences, "A saddle
%     joint is an articulation ... This joint type is biaxial. One example is
%     the joint formed by the trapezium and 1st metacarpal bone." No single
%     sentence names the saddle joint and its bones: the bone sentence says
%     only "One example", and the biaxial sentence sits between the two.
%
% The envelope is now a framing sentence from the page, which names no joint
% type and no bone:
%
%     "Joint classifications offer a broad understanding of joints."
%
% Measured 2026-09-15 on the raw page (HTTP 200; a nonsense path on the same
% host returns 404), with inline tags removed without a space and only ASCII
% whitespace collapsed: the pivot sentence, the knuckles sentence, the
% three-sentence saddle span and the envelope each occur exactly once, each
% inside a `<p>`. Every row's page is the envelope's, so no row restates a
% `locator` line or `trust` (ADJ-TABLES §4).
% ============================================================================

table joint_formed_by {
    columns joint_type, bone

    row (pivot, atlas) {
        source "The atlantoaxial joint, formed by the 1st (atlas) and 2nd (axis) cervical vertebrae, is a pivot joint."
    }
    row (pivot, axis) {
        source "The atlantoaxial joint, formed by the 1st (atlas) and 2nd (axis) cervical vertebrae, is a pivot joint."
    }
    row (condyloid, distal_metacarpals) {
        source "Examples of condyloid joints are the knuckles, formed by the distal metacarpals and proximal phalanges of the medial 4 fingers."
    }
    row (condyloid, proximal_phalanges) {
        source "Examples of condyloid joints are the knuckles, formed by the distal metacarpals and proximal phalanges of the medial 4 fingers."
    }
    row (saddle, trapezium) {
        source "A saddle joint is an articulation between 2 saddle-shaped bones, which are concave in one direction and convex in another. This joint type is biaxial. One example is the joint formed by the trapezium and 1st metacarpal bone."
    }
    row (saddle, first_metacarpal) {
        source "A saddle joint is an articulation between 2 saddle-shaped bones, which are concave in one direction and convex in another. This joint type is biaxial. One example is the joint formed by the trapezium and 1st metacarpal bone."
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE WAS THE PIVOT ROW'S OWN SENTENCE, the primary source of
    % all six answers, and the condyloid and saddle bones were read across
    % two table-level `cites`. Each row now carries its own span, and the
    % envelope is a framing sentence that names no joint type and no bone.
    % Every row overrides it, so it reaches no answer.
    source "Joint classifications offer a broad understanding of joints."
    locator "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507893/"
    trust authoritative
}
